---
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { getLegalPage, legalHref } from '../../lib/legal';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/seo';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const page = getLegalPage('privacy-policy')!;
---

<LegalLayout
	page={page}
	intro="This policy describes practices verified against the current Digital Catholic codebase and public hosting setup."
>
	<div class="callout">
		<p>
			Digital Catholic is a fully static website. As of the last update of this policy, the project does
			<strong>not</strong> use analytics services, advertising trackers, or first-party tracking cookies.
			We have not added a cookie consent banner because no non-essential tracking cookies are set by the
			site itself.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>1. Who we are</h2>
	<p>
		Digital Catholic (“we”, “us”) is an independent educational and devotional website maintained as an
		open project. Contact:
		<a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>
		(also listed on the <a href={withBase('/contact')}>Contact</a> page).
	</p>

	<h2>2. Information collected from visitors</h2>
	<p>
		The Website does not provide user accounts, comment forms, newsletter signup forms, or other
		server-side forms that store personal information in a Digital Catholic database. We do not operate
		a first-party database of visitors.
	</p>
	<p>Personal information may reach us only if you choose to provide it, for example by:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Sending an email to the maintainer address published on the Contact page.</li>
		<li>Opening a public issue or pull request on the project’s GitHub repository.</li>
		<li>Using a web-based email compose link (such as Gmail) that you choose to open from the Contact or search interfaces.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>3. Automatically collected technical information</h2>
	<p>
		When you visit a static website, your browser typically sends technical data such as IP address, user
		agent, requested URL, referrer (if any), and timing information to the host that serves the files.
		Digital Catholic does not run its own application server that logs or analyses this data for marketing
		purposes.
	</p>

	<h2>4. IP addresses and hosting logs</h2>
	<p>
		The production site is deployed to <strong>GitHub Pages</strong>. GitHub, as the hosting provider, may
		process technical request data (including IP addresses) in accordance with its own privacy policy and
		security practices. Digital Catholic’s maintainers do not operate a separate analytics product on top
		of those logs. For GitHub’s practices, see GitHub’s published privacy documentation.
	</p>
	<p>
		If the project is also run from a Docker/nginx or similar self-hosted mirror, that host’s access logs
		may similarly record standard web-server request metadata under the operator’s control.
	</p>

	<h2>5. Contact email information</h2>
	<p>
		If you email us, we receive the content of your message, your email address, and any details you
		include. We use that information only to respond to your enquiry, assess corrections or copyright
		requests, or administer the project. We do not sell contact details.
	</p>

	<h2>6. GitHub Pages hosting considerations</h2>
	<p>
		Pages are built ahead of time and served as static files. There is no Digital Catholic login system and
		no server-side session for browsing. Deployment configuration for GitHub Pages is part of the public
		repository workflows. Visitors should also review GitHub’s terms and privacy notices for the platform
		that hosts the live site and the source repository.
	</p>

	<h2>7. Analytics and cookies</h2>
	<p>
		<strong>Analytics:</strong> The codebase does not integrate Google Analytics, Plausible, Umami, Meta
		Pixel, tag managers, or similar products.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Cookies:</strong> The site does not intentionally set first-party cookies for tracking. The
		client-side search feature fetches a local <code>search-index.json</code> file from the same origin; that
		request does not require a consent cookie. Essential browser or CDN/platform cookies, if any, would be
		controlled by the hosting platform rather than by Digital Catholic application code.
	</p>
	<p>
		If analytics or non-essential cookies are introduced in the future, this Privacy Policy will be updated
		and any legally required consent mechanism will be considered before activation.
	</p>

	<h2>8. Third-party services</h2>
	<p>Depending on how you use the site, third parties may include:</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>GitHub / GitHub Pages</strong> — hosting of the live site and the public source repository.</li>
		<li><strong>Email providers</strong> — if you contact us by email (for example Gmail or your own mail client).</li>
		<li><strong>External linked websites</strong> — Church, educational, or other sites you choose to open; their practices are their own (see the <a href={legalHref('external-links')}>External Links Disclaimer</a>).</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Fonts are served from system font stacks bundled in site CSS; the project does not load Google Fonts or
		other remote font CDNs. Images and scripts for pages are served from the site’s own static assets,
		aside from destinations you deliberately navigate to.
	</p>

	<h2>9. Data storage and retention</h2>
	<p>
		Email and GitHub correspondence are retained only as long as reasonably needed to handle your request
		and maintain the project, unless a longer period is required by law or dispute resolution. We do not
		maintain a marketing list from Website visits.
	</p>

	<h2>10. International data handling</h2>
	<p>
		GitHub and common email providers may process data in multiple countries. If you contact us or visit
		the GitHub-hosted site from outside Australia, your information may be handled in those locations
		subject to the providers’ terms. We do not claim that a particular cross-border transfer mechanism
		applies beyond what those providers publish.
	</p>

	<h2>11. Privacy and data protection rights</h2>
	<p>
		Australian privacy law, including the Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) and the Australian Privacy Principles,
		may apply depending on the circumstances of the organisation or individual operating the project
		(including thresholds and exemptions). This policy does <strong>not</strong> assert that the Privacy
		Act does or does not apply to Digital Catholic in every case.
	</p>
	<p>
		Regardless of formal applicability, you may ask us what personal information we hold about you from
		direct correspondence, request correction of inaccurate contact details we hold, or ask us to delete
		emails where practicable. We will respond in good faith within a reasonable time.
	</p>
	<p>
		We do not claim GDPR certification or compliance in this policy. If you are in a jurisdiction with
		specific privacy rights, tell us which rights you wish to exercise when you contact us.
	</p>

	<h2>12. Privacy complaints</h2>
	<p>
		If you have a privacy concern, please contact
		<a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>
		first so we can try to resolve it. You may also have the right to lodge a complaint with the Office of
		the Australian Information Commissioner (OAIC) or another competent regulator, depending on your
		circumstances.
	</p>

	<h2>13. Children’s privacy</h2>
	<p>
		The Website is a general educational resource. We do not knowingly collect personal information from
		children through site forms, because we do not operate such forms. Pastoral and parental guidance
		should accompany children’s use of online religious materials.
	</p>

	<h2>14. Changes to this policy</h2>
	<p>
		We may update this Privacy Policy when hosting, contact methods, or site features change. The “Last
		updated” date will be revised when changes are published.
	</p>

	<h2>15. Contact</h2>
	<p>
		Privacy questions:
		<a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>
		· <a href={withBase('/contact')}>Contact page</a>
		· Repository: <a href={SITE.repoUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
	</p>
</LegalLayout>
